 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "Which khao soi on the Chiang Mai Michelin list should I try?",
   "a": "Khao Soi Mae Sai is the name people mention most on the list, with soft noodles and a rich, deep curry broth. Go around midday before it sells out."
  },
  {
   "q": "Where do I go for real Northern Thai food?",
   "a": "Huen Muan Jai and Huan Soontaree serve traditional Northern spreads, while Gongkham and Euang Kam Sai are local favorites for the same regional cooking."
  },
  {
   "q": "What are the cheapest Michelin picks in Chiang Mai?",
   "a": "Street eats like Lung Khajohn Wat Ket, Sanpakoi Kanomjeen, and Roti Pa Day fill you up for pocket change, usually well under a hundred baht."
  },
  {
   "q": "How should a first-timer plan a Chiang Mai eating route?",
   "a": "Start the morning with Khao Soi Mae Sai, snack around the Wat Ket area at Lung Khajohn Wat Ket, then close the day with a Northern spread at Huen Muan Jai or Huan Soontaree."
  },
  {
   "q": "Does Chiang Mai have any Michelin-starred restaurants?",
   "a": "Not in 2026. Chiang Mai has no starred spots, but it carries 18 Bib Gourmand restaurants plus 18 Selected, making it a stronghold for great, affordable eating."
  }
